  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on a newly developed satellite-based counting method as a proxy for actual ground-level truck traffic, which may introduce unmeasured detection errors across different built environments.",
      "what": "This study mapped the spatial distribution of heavy-duty trucks and containers across 746 census tracts in southern California using a satellite-based truck-counting method that fuses satellite imagery with public GIS data. The researchers analyzed how truck and container density relates to warehouse density, PM2.5 concentrations, environmental burdens, and sociodemographic factors. They found that warehouse density, PM2.5 levels, and the presence of hazardous waste facilities are strongly and positively associated with truck density (p < 0.0001), while population density and the proportion of White residents are inversely associated.",
      "why": "It demonstrates a new satellite-based data source for mapping freight traffic at a census-tract scale, proving that warehouse siting directly correlates with localized truck density and compounding environmental injustices for minority populations."
    }
  },
